<subsection xml:id="subsec-mod">
  <title>The mod function</title>

  <p>
    The <pf>mod</pf> function computes the remainder after division. It is useful for determining if a number is even or odd, among other applications. For example, <pf>mod(n, 2)</pf> returns 0 if <pf>n</pf> is even and 1 if <pf>n</pf> is odd.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="quiz-for-loop-conceptual-6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which expression checks whether <pf>n</pf> is even?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><pf>mod(n,2) == 0</pf></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Even numbers leave remainder 0 when divided by 2.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><pf>mod(n,2) == 1</pf></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Remainder 1 identifies odd numbers, not even numbers.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><pf>n/2 == 0</pf></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>This is only true when <pf>n</pf> is zero.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><pf>n - 2 == 0</pf></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>This checks only whether <pf>n</pf> equals 2.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>
  
</subsection>